[
{
"content_id": "27190",
"reference_id": 26332,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "vain, vanity",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "vain vanity",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "None",
"content": "<h2>Definition:</h2>\n<p>The terms “vain” and “vanity” describe something that is useless or extremely temporary.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the Old Testament, idols are sometimes described as “vain” things that are worthless and cannot do anything.</li>\n<li>If something is done “in vain,” it means that there the effort or action did not accomplish what was intended. The phrase “in vain” might be translated in various ways, including: “without result;” “with no result;” “for no reason;” “for no purpose,” or “with no purpose.”</li>\n<li>Depending on the context, the term “vain” could be translated as “empty,” “useless,” “hopeless,” “worthless,” “meaningless,” etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(See also: <span data-bnType=\"resourceReference\" data-resourceId=\"25728\" data-resourceType=\"UWTranslationWords\">false god</span>, worthy)</p>\n<h2>Bible References:</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>1 Corinthians 15:1–2</li>\n<li>1 Samuel 25:21–22</li>\n<li>2 Peter 2:18</li>\n<li>Isaiah 45:19</li>\n<li>Jeremiah 2:29–31</li>\n<li>Matthew 15:9</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Word Data:</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Strong’s: H1891, H1892, H2600, H7307, H7385, H7387, H7723, H8193, H8267, H8414, G09450, G15000, G27560, G27580, G27610, G31510, G31520, G31530, G31550</li>\n</ul>",
